---
description: Always let the user review changes before committing them to git
alwaysApply: true
---

# Review before commit

Never run `git commit` right after making code/content changes without the
user explicitly reviewing and approving them first.

- After making changes, summarize what changed (files touched, what/why)
  and stop - do not commit in the same turn.
- Only commit once the user has looked at the diff and given explicit
  go-ahead (e.g. "commit this", "looks good, commit", "yes, go ahead").
- If the user asks you to "implement X and commit it" in one request, still
  pause for review before the commit step - implement, summarize the diff,
  and wait for confirmation rather than committing automatically.
- This applies to every commit, not just the first one in a session -
  don't assume approval carries over to later, unrelated changes.
